// Exercise 5.2 - one leaf stops being taken on faith.
//
// ../bricks/graceful.jd says "The test dataset is trustworthy" and stops there, as a leaf.
// That is a large thing to assert without argument. ../bricks/trustworthy.jd is the
// argument for it, written separately.
//
// Check your work with:   jpipe diagnostic -i 02_refine.jd

load "../bricks/graceful.jd"
load "../bricks/trustworthy.jd"

// TODO write a justification named `graceful_deep` that refines `graceful` with
// `trustworthy`:
//
//   justification <name> is refine(<base>, <detail>) {
//       hook: "<element id>"
//   }
//
// The hook is an element id *from the base*, naming the node that gets replaced. Open
// graceful.jd and find the id of the leaf about the test dataset.
//
// The result needs a new name. A model cannot refine itself into its own name, and the
// compiler will say so plainly if you try.
//
// Two things worth doing on purpose once it works:
//
//   1. Put a wrong id in the hook and read the error. Then try an id that exists in
//      `trustworthy` rather than in `graceful`, and notice you get the same error. The
//      hook only ever looks in the base.
//
//   2. Compare the element counts of `graceful` and `graceful_deep` in the model summary.
//      One node changed kind. Which one, and to what?
